{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 47, :comments ["<b>the family of a ger</b> — this is the gentile. When Scripture says “to the eiker,” this refers to one sold to idolatry itself, to serve it — not as divinity, but to chop wood for it and draw water for it. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 25:47) from Torat Kohanim (chapter 8, halakhah 1). If so, Scripture should say “or to the eiker and the family of a ger.” And it called idolatry “eiker” because a person is obligated to uproot it and tear it out by the roots. And Onkelos (Onkelos on Leviticus 25:47) translated it as “to an Aramaean,” which is the correct interpretation. Scripture mentioned one sold to a ger toshav among us, and one sold to the eiker of a ger's family — which is the gentile himself — and this is from the language of “eikar shorshehei” (its stump of roots) (Daniel 4:12). And it says “the family of a ger” because the ger or toshav whose means sufficed among us has brought it about through the brother who is with him, that he may be sold to the eiker of his family. And Scripture commanded that redemption shall be his, and this is a positive commandment for every Israelite who redeems him; and afterward (Leviticus 25:49) Scripture commanded concerning the relatives, who take precedence. And the reason for the commandment is clear: so that he not be absorbed among them and learn from their deeds, for in the case of other sold persons, redemption by relatives is optional. Therefore Scripture says here (Leviticus 25:55), “For the children of Israel are My servants, My servants they are” — to say: even though the ger toshav and the eiker are not obligated in the commandment of the Jubilee, they shall not acquire My servants for themselves as slaves in perpetuity."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "In Torat Kohanim, the phrase לעקר is read as referring to sale for idolatrous service; Ramban first cites this reading, then endorses Onkelos's rendering “to an Aramaean,” understanding עקר as cognate with Aramaic עקר (root), i.e., the ethnic stock of the ger, meaning a born gentile."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:00:39.604192350Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}